#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S4/D4 (G11): dynamic field access, and the D5 seam.
;;
;; `(.x p)` with `p : any` had exhausted both static routes -- there is no
;; record field on `any`, and no typeclass instance for `.x` -- so it was "no
;; typeclass method found for 'x'".  In Saffron the receiver's type is whatever
;; arrived, so the field is resolved then, against the value's own constructor.
;;
;; `get-x` below is called with two UNRELATED struct types.  That is the point:
;; duck typing, where the field name is the whole contract, and it is what a
;; static `.x` cannot express without a typeclass.
;;
;; The second half covers the Saffron -> Turmeric SEAM (D5).  `takes-int` has a
;; real signature, and a Saffron caller holds `any`.  D5 weighed three options
;; and chose gradual typing with a RUNTIME CHECK: rejecting unless the caller
;; annotates is safe and unusable, and erasing -- passing the payload word
;; through unchecked -- turns a type error into a memory-safety bug, because
;; the word is whatever the box happened to hold.  The check reuses the node
;; `(cast x T)` lowers to, so a wrong type panics with the ordinary cast
;; message rather than reinterpreting the payload.  The panic case is in
;; errors/saffron-seam-wrong-type; this fixture asserts the value passes
;; through when it IS the right type.
;;
;; S5 compiles the field read as an if/else chain over the box id, one arm per
;; type in the program that HAS a field of this name -- the emit-time analogue
;; of the interpreter's run-time name scan.  Worth stating plainly: the
;; interpreter's version is OPEN (any struct with the right field works) and the
;; compiled one is CLOSED over the program, which is inherent to compiling
;; rather than a shortcut.  For a single program the two sets coincide, which is
;; why this fixture can assert the same output from both.

(defstruct Pt   [x : int  y : int])
(defstruct Name [x : cstr])

(defn get-x [p] (.x p))

(defn takes-int [n : int] : int (+ n 1))
(defn dyn [v] v)

(defn main [] : int
  ;; One function, two unrelated receivers, resolved by field name.
  (println (get-x (make-struct Pt 3 4)))
  (println (get-x (make-struct Name "hi")))
  ;; A dynamic value crossing into a typed callee, checked at the seam.
  (println (takes-int (dyn 41)))
  0)
